ts express  their individualism and the way their  efforts pan out reflects a stark  ideological difference both movies  depict an ant colony as a highly  regimented society in which each  individual ant is a worker a member of  the nobility or in the case of ants a  soldier in a bug's life the work is  largely agricultural gathering food for  the winter months that's it that's it  good you're doing great there you go  there you go this is because the ants  have to gather enough food not only for  themselves but also for a roving gang of  grasshoppers this is loosely based off  one of aesop's fables the ant colony in  a bug's life thus resembles a mostly  pre-industrial colonial society where  the colonists the grasshoppers are  extracting natural resources from the  ants through the exploitation of their  labor that's not unlike the way colonial  powers like france exploited the  resources and labor of algeria in  exchange the grasshoppers offer a vague  veil of protection from other scary bugs  who might do the ants even more harm  listen if you don't keep your end of the  bargain then i can't guarantee your  safety here the exploitation of the ants  is being painted as being in their best  interest this too is not unlike the way  colonial powers like france justify  their colonization by saying that they  were actually acting in the algerian's  best interest specifically by quote  unquote civilizing them when they were  in actuality violently oppressing them  importantly a bug's life also shows a  society that's steeped in tradition and  highly resistant to change with flick  gone you can't mess anything up when  flick introduces a machine that can make  gathering food go faster he's dismissed  and mocked this is my new idea for  harvesting grain no more picking  individual kernels you can just cut down  the entire stock  we don't have time for this exactly this  lightly evokes some of the growing pains  of industrialization where machines  drastically changed the skills needed to  perform tasks that humans had been doing  for centuries in contrast ants depicts  the colony as an explicitly industrial  society complete with unreliable public  transit workers are made to constantly  dig tunnels haul dirt and form wrecking  balls out of their own bodies the entire  colony resembles a modern factory floor  complete with motivational posters that  discourage idleness the workers perhaps  uncoincidentally resemble miners an  industry famous for widespread labor  strikes and brutal crackdowns on them  the other faction of the colony in ants  is the military soldiers in the society  enjoy a higher social status than the  lowly workers why'd i have to be born a  worker  you soldiers get all the glory plus you  get to go out in the world you know you  meet interesting insects you get to kill  them even though the selection process  to determine their status is comically  arbitrary  uh worker  soldier  this emphasis on a strong military along  with a highly productive workforce  resembles how a lot of industrial  societies organize themselves after the  first world war the mass mobilization of  an unprecedented number of military  personnel occurred alongside an equally  unprecedented increase in industrial  production this created nations where  nearly every citizen was involved in at  least one of those aspects of society  this made for even stronger national  identities we see this same sense of  nationalism throughout the society  depicted in ants where every member  exists primarily to contribute to the  colony rather than to realize their own  ambitions a soldier knows that the life  of an individual ant doesn't matter  what matters is the colony from these  two highly homogenous societies we get  our two heroes flick and z both  exemplify individualism in the face of  an overwhelmingly conformist environment  for flick this is expressed through his  ingenuity and inventiveness allah his  new harvesting machine but his genius is  unappreciated by the ruling class who  wants him to simply fall in line and  contribute to the common cause of the  colony in this way flake seems to evoke  the ideals of author ein rand her novel  the fountainhead centers around an  architect who also struggles to bring  his new ideas to fruition in an  overwhelmingly conformist society near  the end of the novel the architect gives  a speech that encapsulates rand's  philosophy of individualism stating the  great creators the thinkers the artists  the scientists the inventors stood alone  against the men of their time every  great new thought was opposed every  great new invention was denounced  according to rand the wealth of nations  was created by these indispensable  innovators and any attempt to stifle  their brilliance was an attack on their  individual liberty this proved a very  convenient philosophy if you were one of  these innovators and that is still  enjoyed by certain space traveling union  busting tax evading gazillionaires today  zee on the other hand is not a man of  the mind at least in the randian sense  he's simply a disaffected worker whose  role in industrial society has rendered  him insignificant the whole system makes  me feel  insignificant  excellent you've made a real  breakthrough i have  yes z  you are insignificant z feels utterly  unfulfilled as a worker for the colony  and longs for a life where he can be his  own insect philosopher karl marx might  have said he was alienated from his work  marx argued that industrial capitalism  not only alienates the worker from the  product of their labor it also alienates  them from their own agency and humanity  reducing the individual to nothing more  than a cog in the grand machine of  industrialism the film is not subtle  about invoking marx's philosophy a  worker can't do anything except work  yeah it's not like we got a choice  unlike its disney pixar counterpart the  dreamworks production is much more  explicit in its political themes it's  the workers who control the means of  production it even takes subtle shots at  randy and philosophy in its imagery  throughout the film we see a statue that  depicts an ant carrying a large ball of  dirt on its back the statue is likely a  direct reference to the statue of atlas  that adorns the cover of rand's seminal  work atlas shrugged but replacing the  celestial sphere carried by the mythical  titan with the ant equivalent of a  boulder also invokes a different greek  myth the punishment of sisyphus who is  made to continuously push a boulder up a  hill  only to have it roll back down once he  gets to the top a symbol of the virtues  of industrialism thus becomes a symbol  of the futility of fruitless labor this  neatly sums up z's outlook on his life  within the colony as well as marx's  critique of industrial capitalism  already we can see how these two heroes  embody almost contradictory philosophies  while still both being motivated by  individualism in the face of conformity  both protagonists embark on pretty  standard heroes quests and both seem to  succeed pretty quickly albeit only  through misleading their fellow ants no  i i just you lied flick you lied to her  you lied to the colony you lied to me  after some humiliation both heroes  eventually take on their oppressors  inspire the masses and ultimately rely  on the strength of their communities and  their sheer numbers to vanquish the bad  guys in this way both protagonists  inspire their colonies to rise up in a  revolutionary act against oppressors  they had long obeyed without question i  know what you're thinking these are the  same movie but they're really not trust  us we're going somewhere with this sure  the two films have different plot twists  different symbolism and different cringy  flirting between worker aunt and  princess we need to talk too much  [Music]  which i  think i'm about to become this strong  silent type this is of course made all  the cringier by the casting but we  digress but what's most interesting to  us are the film's two conclusions about  these acts of revolution and the new  world orders they result in a bug's life  ends with the ants overthrowing their  colonial oppressors adopting flake's  harvesting technology and generally  living happily ever after this  reiterates the idea of great men in the  randian tradition who should be listened  to by virtue of them being great the  subtle hints of inequality go further  the princess gets her crown suggesting  that the rigid order within the anthill  will remain largely the same even if the  ants are now more efficient gatherers  who can sneak in an occasional siesta  ants on the other hand does something  arguably more interesting notably the  villainous warrior ant's evil plan is to  use the anthill's new expanded tunnel to  flood the anthill and drown the lowly  workers here the supposed progress of  expanding the anthill and making the  colonies stronger relies on the  destruction of all the workers who made  it possible in the first place while  technology in a bug's life makes things  indisputably better progress in ants  ends up threatening the lives of all the  workers this too is marxist and its  condemnation of endless economic  progress at the expense of the workers  who enable that progress ants seems to  get that you can't disconnect technology  and industry from political power  ultimately a bug's life suggests that  the class order of your average ant hill  is perfectly fine so long as you're not  being oppressed by creatures twice your  size monarchy good colonialism bad on  the other hand ants suggest that the  system itself is fundamentally broken a  bug's life depicts outside forces i.e  grasshoppers as the problem but ants  suggest that class structures within the  colony are to blame for oppression  adding to that a bug's life never really  questions the contention that the  princess's life is more valuable than  that of a worker hand indeed the colony  watches flick get beaten up by a very  scary grasshopper for a while without  intervening it's only when the princess  throws herself in front of him that they  all rally in contrast the conflict in  ants is fundamentally about the  disparities in power and opportunities  afforded to ants based on their  birthright as workers soldier or  nobility z perhaps not coincidentally  the last letter of the alphabet stands  up to the princess and demands to be  treated with respect i order you to stop  worker hey i got a name okay it's z and  ultimately the film champions the equal  importance of every ant in contributing  to the survival of the colony this  emphasis on the inherent value and  equality of every individual is in line  with the tradition of marxist humanism  or in this case antism but interestingly  or depressingly whether or not you stand  marx ants doesn't do what a bug's life  does it doesn't really offer a solution  to the problems faced by its anthill in  the conclusion all the main character  says is there you have to your average  boy meets girl boy likes girl boy  changes underlying social order story  however the film never really tells us  what the existing social order was  replaced with  z does note that the anthill has been  rebuilt and now has an indoor swimming  pool while this is probably a joke about  the flood in the previous scene you  could charitably interpret it to mean  that the workers might get some more  leisure time going forward kind of like  how you can play ping pong at your  60-hour a week startup job still we  don't get a definitive conclusion about  how you can avoid oppressive social  structures entirely in the context of an  anthill or allegorically in the context  of human society in this way although  ants is certainly the more radical of  the two films it arguably does itself a  disservice by failing to show that there  is indeed another way in a way the  conclusion arguably reflects an overall  lack of political imagination in the  west to posit an alternative to  industrial capitalism or as the line  that is often attributed to frederick  jameson goes it's easier to imagine the  end of the world than the end of  capitalism ants claims that the ants  rallied to build a new world but fails  to imagine what that new world might  look like but what do you think are we  over analyzing a couple of fun silly  kids movies about ants or can we find  surprisingly deep meditations on modern  life and even the smallest of places  let us know in the comments big thanks  to our patrons for all your support and  be sure to check out our podcast crush  that subscribe button like it's a fully  conscious self-actualized ant and don't  forget to ring that bell and as always  thanks for watching  later  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

